The proposed solution contains a critical algebra error. In Section 3, from abc = a+b+c it subtracts a to get **bc = b+c**, which is wrong (it should be a(bc−1) = b+c). This leads to the false solution 422 (indeed 4·2·2 = 16 ≠ 8) and invalid steps afterward. The "permutation" additions and the double-counting/332 corrections are also erroneous — 332 fails the condition (3·3·2 = 18 ≠ 8), and dividing by 3 makes no sense for listing distinct numbers.

The correct analysis: the three-digit solutions are the permutations of digits 1, 2, 3 (since a(bc−1) = b+c gives, for a=1, (b−1)(c−1)=2 → {b,c}={2,3}; a=2 and a=3 yield only these same permutations). These are 123, 132, 213, 231, 312, 321, summing to 1332. Together with one-digit numbers (sum 45) and 22, the total is 45 + 22 + 1332 = 1399.

